plan.description:
`GET /api/<resource>/meta`: which fields play a part in every row (`roles`: the id, the title, the picture, the version); and per field its key and label; its type: its kind, the settings that kind takes (an option field's options and their attributes, such as a status's group; the resource a ref field points at) and whether it holds a list; its format; and what a query and a write may do with it (`query`, and `write`, which holds its default). A kind is a registered name, as a format is: bh registers text, url, number, boolean, date, datetime, option, ref and json, and an app may add its own.

```ts
/** GET /api/<resource>/meta */
interface ResourceMeta {
  // 'items': the path segment after /api/
  resource: string;
  // what a row is called, one and many: {one: 'Item', other: 'Items'}, so a
  // screen built from the metadata says "New item", "1 item", "12 items"
  label: { one: string; other: string };
  // which field plays each part in every row
  roles: ResourceRoles;
  // in the order a form or a table shows them at first
  fields: FieldMeta[];
}

/** The fields that play a part in every row, each by its key. */
interface ResourceRoles {
  // the field that names a row: 'id'
  idField: string;
  // the field a row reads by in one line wherever it's named (a link, a
  // picker, a group), often a templated one: 'name' → 'TIX-12 Fix the login'
  titleField: string;
  // the url field holding the row's picture, shown beside its title: 'photo',
  // or a templated field ('/api/users/{id}/avatar'); none, or an empty value
  // → no picture
  imageField?: string;
  // the field a write's `version` is checked against: 'updatedAt'; none → no
  // check (TK-11.3)
  versionField?: string;
}

/**
 * Text with field paths in braces: '{project}-{number} {title}', '{name}'.
 * A placeholder shows its value as text: an option its label, a ref the
 * title of the row it points at, a formatted field in its format, a many field
 * its values joined by ', ', an empty value nothing. The result is trimmed
 * and its runs of spaces fold into one.
 * In a templated field of kind `url`, each placeholder's value is
 * URL-encoded, so a space or a slash can't break the address, and an empty
 * one leaves the field empty.
 * '{{' and '}}' are a brace.
 */
type Template = string;

/**
 * A field, of any kind. Its kind (`type.kind`) is a name in the kind registry
 * (KindSpec):
 * one of bh's nine or an app's own. The kind says what the field's value is,
 * which operators filter it, whether it sorts, groups and holds a list, and
 * what settings it takes.
 */
interface FieldMeta {
  // 'status': unique in its resource, without a dot (a dot starts a path,
  // TK-11.2.1)
  key: string;
  // 'Status'
  label: string;
  // what it holds (FieldType)
  type: FieldType;
  // how its value shows: a registered format that takes its kind; the kind's
  // default when absent. bh's default renderer for the kind uses it (TK-79.3),
  // so a field shows the same in every table, list, card and form unless a
  // template says otherwise.
  format?: FieldFormat;
  // a templated field's value: the server fills it on every row from the
  // row's other fields, '{project}-{number} {title}'; it is never written
  // (its write.create and write.update are false)
  template?: Template;
  // what a query may do with it (TK-11.2)
  query: FieldQuery;
  // what a write may do with it, and its default (TK-11.3)
  write: FieldWrite;
}

/**
 * What a field holds: a value of its kind, or a list of them. Its kind checks
 * it as one object (KindSpec): {kind: 'option', many: true, settings:
 * {options: […]}}.
 */
interface FieldType {
  // a registered kind: 'option'
  kind: FieldKind;
  // what its kind takes besides these keys, checked by the kind's settings
  // schema: an option field's options, a ref field's resource (KindSettings)
  settings?: { [key: string]: Json };
  // holds a list: labels, several assignees; only where its kind allows it
  many?: true;
}

// a registered kind's name: one of bh's (BaseKind) or an app's own
type FieldKind = string;

/**
 * What the metadata knows of a registered kind, to check a field of it and
 * the queries and writes that name it. An app registers its own beside bh's
 * nine, with its SQL for the adapter (TK-12.2) and its default renderer
 * (TK-79.3).
 */
interface KindSpec {
  // 'option'
  name: string;
  // its settings' valibot schema; none → it takes none
  settings?: v.GenericSchema;
  // its value's valibot schema, given a field's `type.settings`: an option's
  // value is one of the field's options
  value: (settings?: { [key: string]: Json }) => v.GenericSchema;
  // a field of it may hold a list
  many: boolean;
  // the most a field of it may allow (FieldQuery): registered operators by
  // name (TK-11.2.2)
  operators: Operator[];
  sort: boolean;
  group: boolean;
  search: boolean;
  // the format a field of it gets when it names none: 'plain', 'date'
  format?: string;
}

/**
 * The kinds bh registers. Their values are KindValue's, their operators
 * TK-11.2.2's and their order TK-11.2.3's; only option and ref take settings
 * (KindSettings).
 *
 *            many  sort  group  search  format
 * text       yes   yes   -      yes     plain
 * url        yes   yes   -      yes     -
 * number     -     yes   -      -       number
 * boolean    -     yes   yes    -       -
 * date       -     yes   -      -       date
 * datetime   -     yes   -      -       datetime
 * option     yes   yes   yes    -       -
 * ref        yes   yes   yes    -       -
 * json       -     -     -      -       -
 */
type BaseKind = keyof KindValue;

/** The settings of bh's kinds that take any. */
interface KindSettings {
  option: {
    // in their order
    options: OptionMeta[];
    // what its options carry besides a label
    attributes?: AttributeMeta[];
  };
  ref: {
    // the resource its ids are rows of: 'users', 'items', 'projects'
    of: string;
  };
}

/**
 * What a query may do with a field (TK-11.2): no more than its kind allows
 * (KindSpec).
 */
interface FieldQuery {
  // the operators it allows, some of its kind's (TK-11.2.2); [] → not
  // filterable
  filter: Operator[];
  sort: boolean;
  group: boolean;
  // a query's `search` looks in it
  search: boolean;
}

/** What a write may do with a field (TK-11.3). */
interface FieldWrite {
  // a create may set it
  create: boolean;
  // a patch may change it
  update: boolean;
  // a create must set it (unless it has a default), and a patch can't clear it
  required: boolean;
  // what a create that leaves it out gets: a value of its kind; a list on a
  // many field
  default?: FieldValue;
}

/**
 * How a field's value shows: a format registered in bh's format registry
 * (TK-79.4), by its name, or by its name with options. Any registered format
 * that takes the field's kind will do, an app's own too: 'bytes', 'relative',
 * { name: 'money', options: { currency: 'EUR' } }.
 */
type FieldFormat = string | { name: string; options: { [key: string]: Json } };

/**
 * What the metadata knows of a registered format, to check a field's: the
 * kinds it takes and its options. Each entry of TK-79.4's registry carries it.
 */
interface FormatSpec {
  // 'money'
  name: string;
  // the kinds it formats: ['number']
  kinds: FieldKind[];
  // its options' valibot schema: { currency: an ISO 4217 code }; none → it
  // takes none
  options?: v.GenericSchema;
}

interface OptionMeta {
  // what a row holds and a filter names: 'in-progress'
  value: string;
  // 'In progress'
  label: string;
  // a bh tone: 'blue', 'success'
  color?: string;
  // its place in the list; sorting and grouping follow it
  order: number;
  // shown on the rows that hold it, offered for no new value
  archived?: true;
  // keyed by AttributeMeta.key: { kind: 'active' }
  attributes?: { [key: string]: Scalar | null };
}

/**
 * What an option field's options carry besides a label: a status's group
 * (tickets' `kind`). A path names one: 'status.kind'. Only an option
 * attribute has options of its own.
 */
type AttributeMeta =
  // { key: 'kind', label: 'Group', kind: 'option', options: [todo, active,
  // blocked, done, dropped] }
  | { key: string; label: string; kind: 'text' | 'number' | 'boolean' }
  | { key: string; label: string; kind: 'option'; options: OptionMeta[] };

/**
 * A row: its fields' values by key; a key that a query's `fields` left out is
 * absent.
 */
type Row = { [key: string]: FieldValue };

/**
 * A field's value is its kind's (KindValue), null when unset; a `many` field's
 * is a list of them, [] when unset.
 */
type FieldValue = Scalar | Scalar[] | Json | null;
type Scalar = string | number | boolean;
type Json = null | Scalar | Json[] | { [key: string]: Json };
// a row's id, as its table has it
type Id = string | number;

/** The value of each of bh's kinds. */
interface KindValue {
  text: string;
  // absolute, with its scheme ('https://…'), or a path from the root
  // ('/api/users/7/avatar'), which a client resolves against the API's address
  url: string;
  number: number;
  boolean: boolean;
  // '2026-10-12': no time, no zone
  date: string;
  // '2026-10-12T09:30:00.000Z': ISO 8601, in UTC
  datetime: string;
  // one of the field's OptionMeta values
  option: string;
  // the id of a row of the resource its `type.settings.of` names
  ref: Id;
  json: Json;
}
```

`titleField` names the field a row reads by, usually a templated one, because a name is often several fields ("TIX-12 Fix the login"). A template holds no code, only placeholders and text, since a stored definition holds no logic (chosen on TK-11), and the server fills it, so every client gets the same text and a templated field can be shown, searched and sorted like any other. A field shows by its `format` and its kind's default renderer in bh (an option as a pill in its color, a ref as the row it points at, by that row's title and picture (a user's avatar and name), a url as a link, a boolean as a check), unless a template (TK-79) says otherwise. `imageField` names a url field too: one that holds the whole address (`photo`), or a templated one built from the row (`'/api/users/{id}/avatar'`).

A field that points at rows of another resource is a `ref`, whatever that resource is: an assignee (`of: 'users'`), an item's parent (`of: 'items'`), its project (`of: 'projects'`). A row holds only ids; a client shows each by the target's `roles.titleField` and `roles.imageField` and picks from its rows, so one picker, chip and avatar serve them all. What's special about people, such as an avatar or "assigned to me", goes by the target being `users`, not by the kind (chosen on TK-11).

A field's kind and its format are names in registries, not lists in this contract: bh registers nine kinds (`KindSpec`) and its formats (TK-79.4), and an app registers its own beside them. So a field is one shape whatever its kind; what a kind adds sits in `type.settings`, which the kind's schema checks, and the metadata checks each field against its kind and its format (TK-11.5).

Two parts of `ResourceMeta` come with the tickets that define their types: `sort`, the order of a query that names none, with `Sort` (TK-11.2.3); and `actions` (TK-11.7).

tickets keeps all of this in its database today: `structure.fields` (key, label, `type`, `config.multiple`, `config.format`), `structure.options` (value, label, position, `kind`) and, per type, `item_type_fields.required`. Its `string` is `text` here. TK-13.1 builds items' metadata from them.

