plan.description:
`POST /api/<resource>/query`. Answers rows, the next cursor, the total when asked, and groups with counts. Every part of the body is optional: `{}` answers the first page of every row, in the resource's own sort.

```ts
/** POST /api/<resource>/query: the body */
interface Query {
  // which rows, by their fields (TK-11.2.1, TK-11.2.2)
  filter?: Filter;
  // which rows, by their text; the filter and the search both hold
  // (TK-11.2.6)
  search?: string;
  // their order; ResourceMeta.sort when absent (TK-11.2.3)
  sort?: Sort[];
  // which page: numbered, or the one after a cursor (TK-11.2.4)
  page?: Page;
  // groups and their counts instead of rows (TK-11.2.5)
  group?: GroupBy;
  // which fields a row carries (TK-11.2.6)
  fields?: string[];
}

/** POST /api/<resource>/query: the answer */
interface QueryResult {
  // [] when the query grouped
  rows: Row[];
  // when the query grouped
  groups?: Group[];
  // the next page's cursor, and the total when asked (TK-11.2.4)
  page: PageResult;
}
```

One project's open items, most urgent first, and how many there are:

```json
{
  "filter": { "all": [
    { "field": "project", "op": "equals", "value": "TIX" },
    { "field": "status.kind", "op": "in", "value": ["todo", "active", "blocked"] }
  ] },
  "sort": [{ "field": "priority", "dir": "asc" }],
  "page": { "take": 100, "total": true }
}
```

The body is checked against the resource's metadata before it runs: an unknown field, an operator, sort or group the field doesn't allow, or a value of the wrong kind answers `invalid`, with the path to each problem (TK-11.4).

subtickets.description:
A filter is data, never code: a view stores it as it is (TK-16), and the server turns it into SQL.

```ts
type Filter =
  // every one holds; { all: [] } keeps every row
  | { all: Filter[] }
  // at least one holds; { any: [] } keeps none
  | { any: Filter[] }
  | { not: Filter }
  | Condition;

interface Condition {
  field: FieldPath;
  // one the field allows: FieldMeta.query.filter (TK-11.2.2)
  op: Operator;
  // what `op` compares with, as its OperatorSpec's operand schema says
  // (TK-11.2.2); absent for empty and notEmpty
  value?: Scalar | Scalar[];
}

/**
 * A field's key: 'status'. Or an option field's key, a dot and one of its
 * attributes: 'status.kind'. One level, no deeper.
 */
type FieldPath = string;
```

Items assigned to user 7 or to no one, that aren't finished:

```json
{ "all": [
  { "not": { "field": "status.kind", "op": "in", "value": ["done", "dropped"] } },
  { "any": [
    { "field": "assignee", "op": "equals", "value": 7 },
    { "field": "assignee", "op": "empty" }
  ] }
] }
```

A path is how a filter reaches what an option means rather than which option it is: `status.kind in [done]` holds for every project's finished statuses, whatever each project calls them. A path filters, sorts and groups like a field of the attribute's kind.

Operators are a registry, as kinds and formats are (chosen on TK-11): bh registers twelve, and an app adds its own. Each kind allows the operators that mean something for it. A field may allow fewer (`FieldMeta.query.filter`), for instance where an operator would need an index the field doesn't have: items-core's reason for opting in per operator (`resources/utils/filter-operators.ts`).

```ts
// a registered operator's name: one of bh's (BaseOperator) or an app's own
type Operator = string;

/**
 * What the query knows of a registered operator, to check a condition that
 * names it (TK-11.5). An app registers its own beside bh's twelve, with its SQL
 * for each adapter it runs on (TK-12.2, TK-13.2) and the value input the
 * FilterBar shows for it (TK-75).
 */
interface OperatorSpec {
  // 'in'
  name: string;
  // how a filter reads it: 'is any of'
  label: string;
  // its operand's valibot schema, given the field's value schema: `in` takes 1
  // to 100 values; none → it takes no operand, as `empty` does
  operand?: (value: v.GenericSchema) => v.GenericSchema;
}

/**
 * The names of bh's twelve. Only names: the operators themselves are entries
 * of the registry, as an app's are (baseOperators, below), just as BaseKind
 * only names bh's nine kinds.
 */
type BaseOperator =
  | 'equals' | 'notEquals'
  | 'in' | 'notIn'
  | 'contains' | 'startsWith'
  // a number's less and greater, a date's before and after
  | 'lt' | 'lte' | 'gt' | 'gte'
  | 'empty' | 'notEmpty';

const EQUALS: BaseOperator[] = ['equals', 'notEquals'];
const IN: BaseOperator[] = ['in', 'notIn'];
const TEXT: BaseOperator[] = ['contains', 'startsWith'];
const RANGE: BaseOperator[] = ['lt', 'lte', 'gt', 'gte'];
const EMPTY: BaseOperator[] = ['empty', 'notEmpty'];

/**
 * bh's twelve as registry entries: each an OperatorSpec, in the same
 * Registry.operators list an app's own join (TK-11.5). Nothing in the query
 * knows an operator except through its entry.
 * { name: 'in', label: 'is any of',
 *   operand: (value) => v.pipe(v.array(value), v.minLength(1), v.maxLength(100)) }
 */
declare const baseOperators: readonly OperatorSpec[];

/**
 * The operators each of bh's kinds allows: its KindSpec's `operators`
 * (TK-11.1). An app's own kind names registered ones, bh's or its own.
 */
const OPERATORS: Record<BaseKind, Operator[]> = {
  text:     [...EQUALS, ...IN, ...TEXT, ...EMPTY],
  url:      [...EQUALS, ...IN, ...TEXT, ...EMPTY],
  number:   [...EQUALS, ...IN, ...RANGE, ...EMPTY],
  date:     [...EQUALS, ...RANGE, ...EMPTY],
  datetime: [...RANGE, ...EMPTY],
  boolean:  ['equals', ...EMPTY],
  option:   [...EQUALS, ...IN, ...EMPTY],
  ref:      [...EQUALS, ...IN, ...EMPTY],
  json:     [...EMPTY],
};

/**
 * What each of bh's operators compares with, on a field whose values are V:
 * its KindValue. Each one's OperatorSpec.operand builds this schema.
 */
interface BaseOperand<V> {
  equals: V;
  notEquals: V;
  // 1 to 100 values
  in: V[];
  notIn: V[];
  // 1 to 200 characters; case is ignored
  contains: string;
  startsWith: string;
  lt: V;
  lte: V;
  gt: V;
  gte: V;
  // no value
  empty: undefined;
  notEmpty: undefined;
}
```

- On a `many` field, `in` holds when the row holds any of the values, `notIn` when it holds none of them, and `empty` when it holds none at all; `equals` and `notEquals` aren't offered.
- `notEquals` and `notIn` keep the rows that have no value, as items-core's do: "status is not done" keeps the items without a status.
- A date compares by day. A datetime compares by instant, so a month is `gte` its first moment and `lt` the next month's.
- tickets' saved filters map onto these: any-of → `in`, none-of → `notIn`, contains → `contains`, empty and not-empty → `empty` and `notEmpty`, kinds → `in` on `status.kind`.
- Not in it yet: a date relative to today ("the last 7 days"), which a saved view needs. It's an operator to register (`inLast`), decided with the views (TK-16).

```ts
interface Sort {
  // a field whose FieldMeta.query.sort is true, or an attribute of an option field
  field: FieldPath;
  dir: 'asc' | 'desc';
}

// its sort part (TK-11.1)
interface ResourceMeta {
  // the order of a query that names none: [{field: 'updatedAt', dir: 'desc'}]
  sort: Sort[];
}
```

How each of bh's kinds orders is the contract's rule, the same in every adapter, never an app's own:

- text and url: alphabetically, case ignored.
- number, date and datetime: by value.
- boolean: false, then true.
- option: by the option's `order`, never its label, so "by status" is the workflow's order.
- ref: by the title of the row it points at (a user's name, an item's title), as the server builds it (TK-12.2).
- json and `many` fields don't sort.
- An app's own kind says whether it sorts (`KindSpec.sort`), and brings its order to the adapter (TK-12.2).
- Rows without a value come last, both ways, as tickets' do today (`apps/web/src/utils/compare-tickets.ts`).
- The id breaks ties: the server adds `{field: roles.idField, dir: 'asc'}` last, so the order is total: the same query answers the same pages while nothing changes, by skip or by cursor (TK-11.2.4), and a cursor never skips or repeats a row.

Pages by skip and take by default: `page: {skip: 40, take: 20}` answers rows 41 to 60, so a table can jump to any page and number its pages. items-core pages this way today (up to 10 000 rows); tickets' board downloads a whole project.

A cursor is the option, for a list that grows as it scrolls. Every answer carries `page.next`, the cursor of the page after it, however that page was asked for; a query that passes it back as `page: {cursor}` starts right after the last row it got. Rows written between two pages then aren't skipped or shown twice unless their place in the order changed, and a late page costs what the first does.

```ts
/**
 * A page, in one of two shapes that never mix: numbered, by skip; or the one
 * after a cursor. The first page of a scrolling list is { take: 20 }.
 */
type Page =
  | {
      // rows to pass over first: 0 when absent
      skip?: number;
      // rows per page: 1 to 500; 100 when absent
      take?: number;
      // also count every row the filter and search keep
      total?: boolean;
    }
  | {
      // the `next` of the page before
      cursor: string;
      // rows per page: 1 to 500; 100 when absent
      take?: number;
    };

/** Where an answered page sits: QueryResult.page. */
interface PageResult {
  // the cursor of the next page; null on the last page
  next: string | null;
  // only when the query asked, so only on a numbered page
  total?: number;
}
```

- A page is one shape or the other, never both (chosen on TK-11): a page with `cursor` beside `skip` or `total` answers `invalid` at `page`, since the schemas check each shape strictly (TK-11.5).
- By skip and take, a row added or removed before the page between two requests shifts every later page by one, so a row can show twice or not at all; and a deep page is slow, since the database walks past every skipped row.
- A cursor is opaque: a client passes back what it got and never builds one.
- A cursor is good only for the query that made it, with the same filter, search and sort; another query's cursor answers `invalid` at `page.cursor`.
- Both page in the sort's total order (TK-11.2.3), so the same query answers the same pages while nothing changes.
- The total is a second count over every matching row, so it comes only with numbered pages, which show it ("page 3 of 12"); a scrolling list doesn't ask for it.

Asked to group, the server answers the groups and their counts, and no rows. A group's rows load when it's opened, by the same query with the group's `filter` added: `filter: {all: [query.filter, group.filter]}`, without `group`. A board's columns, a list's sections and tickets' project stats (TK-15.2, grouped by `status.kind`) are all this.

```ts
interface GroupBy {
  // an option, ref or boolean field whose FieldMeta.query.group is true, or an
  // attribute: 'status.kind'
  field: FieldPath;
  // the groups in the field's sort order (TK-11.2.3); asc when absent
  dir?: 'asc' | 'desc';
  // also answer the options no row holds, with count 0: a board's empty columns
  all?: boolean;
}

interface Group {
  // the value its rows hold; null for the rows that hold none
  key: Scalar | null;
  // its rows that the filter and search keep
  count: number;
  // what picks its rows: equals the key, in [key] on a many field, empty for
  // null
  filter: Filter;
}
```

- A row of a `many` field counts in the group of each value it holds, so the counts can add up to more than the total.
- One field at a time: grouping by two (project, then status) isn't in it.
- A group's label comes from the metadata (`OptionMeta.label`) or, for a ref, from the resource its `of` names: the fields its `roles.titleField` and `roles.imageField` name.

```ts
// its search and fields part (TK-11.2)
interface Query {
  // 1 to 200 characters: keeps the rows where a field with FieldMeta.query.search
  // contains it, case ignored
  search?: string;
  // the keys each row carries; every field when absent. The id and the fields
  // its `roles` name always come, so a row can be named.
  fields?: string[];
}
```

- Search and the filter both hold.
- The term is matched whole, as items-core's search is (`%term%` over its searchable columns): "login fix" doesn't find "fix login". Words, ranking and highlighting aren't in it.
- `fields` names keys, not paths; an unknown key answers `invalid`.

plan.description:
`GET /:id`, `POST`, `PATCH /:id`, `DELETE /:id`, with an optional version check (tickets' `expectedUpdatedAt`). Bulk later.

```http
GET    /api/<resource>/:id                       → 200 Row
POST   /api/<resource>                Create     → 201 Row
PATCH  /api/<resource>/:id            Patch      → 200 Row
DELETE /api/<resource>/:id?version=…             → 204
```

```ts
/**
 * POST's body: the new row's values; only fields with FieldMeta.write.create, and
 * every required one without a default (FieldMeta.write.default).
 */
interface Create {
  values: { [key: string]: FieldValue };
}

/**
 * PATCH's body: the values that change. A key left out keeps its value; null,
 * or [] on a many field, clears it.
 */
interface Patch {
  values: { [key: string]: FieldValue };
  // the row's version field (ResourceMeta.roles.versionField) as it was read
  version?: string;
}
```

- A write answers the whole row as it is after it, so a client puts it in its cache without reading it again.
- With a `version`, a row that changed since answers 409 `conflict` and nothing is written; without one, the last write wins. DELETE takes its version in the URL, as it has no body. tickets checks `expectedUpdatedAt` this way today (`lockItem`, `apps/api/src/command/item/helpers.ts`).
- An `Idempotency-Key` header (a uuid) makes a write safe to repeat: the same key answers the first answer again. tickets passes it on as the command's `commandId`.
- Values sit under `values` so that a resource can have a field called `version`.
- A write is checked like a query: a field it may not set, a required one missing or a value of the wrong kind answers `invalid`.

`{error: {code, message, issues?}}`.

```ts
/** Every error's body, whatever its status. */
interface ErrorBody {
  error: {
    code: ErrorCode;
    // for people: 'item changed since you loaded it — refresh and retry'
    message: string;
    // an invalid answer's: one per problem
    issues?: Issue[];
  };
}

type ErrorCode =
  // 400: the body, a parameter or a cursor is wrong; `issues` says where
  | 'invalid'
  // 404: no such row, or no such resource
  | 'not_found'
  // 409: the version is stale, or a value that must be unique is taken
  | 'conflict'
  // 500: nothing the caller can fix; the message says no more
  | 'internal';

interface Issue {
  // where in the body: ['filter', 'all', 1, 'value']
  path: (string | number)[];
  // 'status has no option "doing"'
  message: string;
}
```

- A client goes by `code`, never by the message.
- An app may add codes (`unauthorized`, `forbidden`); a client that doesn't know one goes by the status.
- `issues` are valibot's, each path flattened to keys and indexes.
- Today tickets answers `{error: 'item not found'}`, a bare string (`apps/api/src/app.ts`); here that's `{error: {code: 'not_found', message: 'item not found'}}`.

Every type on this page is a valibot schema first, and its TypeScript type is inferred from it (`v.InferOutput`), so the two can't drift. Two layers: the shapes, the same for every resource; and the shapes checked against one resource's metadata and the registered kinds and formats.

```ts
import type * as v from 'valibot';
import type { JSONSchema7 } from 'json-schema';
import type { OpenAPIV3_1 } from 'openapi-types';

/* ── The shapes, for any resource ── */

export declare const ResourceMeta: v.GenericSchema<ResourceMeta>;
export declare const Query: v.GenericSchema<Query>;
export declare const QueryResult: v.GenericSchema<QueryResult>;
export declare const Filter: v.GenericSchema<Filter>; // recursive: v.lazy
export declare const Create: v.GenericSchema<Create>;
export declare const Patch: v.GenericSchema<Patch>;
export declare const ErrorBody: v.GenericSchema<ErrorBody>;

/* ── The registered kinds, formats and operators ── */

/** What an app runs with: bh's kinds, formats and operators, and its own. */
export interface Registry {
  kinds: readonly KindSpec[];
  formats: readonly FormatSpec[];
  operators: readonly OperatorSpec[];
}

/** bh's nine kinds (TK-11.1); its formats come with @bh/template (TK-79.4). */
export declare const baseKinds: readonly KindSpec[];

/** bh's twelve operators (TK-11.2.2). */
export declare const baseOperators: readonly OperatorSpec[];

/* ── The shapes checked against one resource ── */

/**
 * The metadata: each field's kind and format registered; its type's settings
 * and many, its write's default and its query no more than its kind allows; its format one that takes
 * its kind, with options its schema accepts.
 */
export declare function metaOf(registry: Registry): v.GenericSchema<ResourceMeta>;

/**
 * Each field exists and allows its operator, sort or group; each operator is
 * registered, and each operand passes its OperatorSpec's schema for the
 * field's kind.
 */
export declare function queryOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Query>;

/** Creatable fields only, and every required one without a default. */
export declare function createOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Create>;

/** Updatable fields only; a required one can't be cleared. */
export declare function patchOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Patch>;

/**
 * Each value of its field's kind. A url is absolute, with its scheme, or a
 * path from the root: one '/', never '//', which would name another host.
 */
export declare function rowOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  registry: Registry,
): v.GenericSchema<Row>;

/* ── For the routes and the docs ── */

/** Through @valibot/to-json-schema. */
export declare function jsonSchemaOf(schema: v.GenericSchema): JSONSchema7;

/** The six routes, their bodies and answers, and ErrorBody on each. */
export declare function openApiOf(
  meta: ResourceMeta,
  basePath: string,
): OpenAPIV3_1.Document;
```

- `FieldMeta` is one shape for every kind. What differs by kind is checked through the registry: a field's `type.settings` by its kind's schema; its `type.many`, `write.default` and `query` by what the kind allows; its `format` by that format's kinds and options schema.
- `metaOf` checks the metadata as a whole too: each of `roles` names a field, `roles.imageField` one of kind `url`, and every path in a templated field's template names one of the row's other fields. A ref field's `of` must name a resource the server serves: that's checked when the server starts, once every resource's metadata is known.
- `Filter` is recursive (`v.lazy`), and its JSON Schema has to come out as a `$ref` to itself. Check that @valibot/to-json-schema does, through its `definitions` option.
- items-core already goes from valibot to JSON Schema to OpenAPI (`packages/backend/server/src/utils/to-fastify-schema.ts`, with `@fastify/swagger`); this ports it.

`records.items.key`, unique across every project, so a key names one item wherever it is written. Every existing item's is today's `PREFIX-number`, and new items in a numbered project get the same. In a plan project a key is set once, when the item is made, and never changes:

- an epic: the prefix, `/E` and the next epic number (`TK/E14`);
- a feature: the prefix and the next feature number in the project (`TK-80`);
- a ticket or a subticket: its parent's key and the next number under it (`TK-80.3`, `TK-11.2.1`).

The API refuses moving a plan item to another parent, since its key says where it sits: it is cancelled and written anew, as today. `GET /api/items/by-key/:key` finds one, and the MCP server and the web address items by key.

The rules `scripts/tasks-check.mjs` adds to the schema, on every write to a plan project:

- a title starts with a verb from the plan scheme's list, today's `VERBS`, which moves into the scheme's settings to be added to there;
- `approved` and `cancelled` never together, and `cancelled` never on a done item;
- a parent whose items are all cancelled is cancelled, not done;
- `waitingOn` names no item: an item is a blocker, with a why;
- an id named in a text is an item's key.

A write that breaks one is refused with 422 and the rule's message, as a status move without a transition is, whether it comes from the web, the MCP server or a script; nothing that breaks a rule is stored, so nothing is left for `pnpm tasks:check` to find. The web shows the message at the field it names. An id named in a text must already be an item's key, so a session writes the item first, or both in one call (TK-83.2's `write_feature`).

Worked out on the server from the stored values, as the viewer and the builder work it out from the files today (`approvedOf`, `cancelledOf` and `waitsOf` in `tasks/viewer.html`; `readiness` in `scripts/tasks-builder.mjs`):

- approved and cancelled, for a parent, from the items without children under it;
- waits: its blockers and its parents' blockers that aren't done, each with its why and the parent it came from; a cancelled blocker is never done;
- blocks: the items that wait on it;
- ready: approved, without children, neither done nor cancelled, not the user's, and waiting on nothing.

The export (TK-85.3), the planner's and builder's waits (TK-86) and the MCP tools read it; TK-87.1 serves it as fields a view can filter by.
